From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 18:39:43 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(web): bound how fast the H.264 decoders open

Since the decoders of surfaces that are gone are closed, a surface that
is created again starts a new stream in a new decoder: its next frame
opens one, and the decoder before it closes. A server could have that
done for every frame. A message of CreateSurface and frame pairs for one
surface had the session hand out 32,768 frames before what waits for the
decoder reached its bound (65,536 commands), and WebCodecsH264Decoder
opened a VideoDecoder, and closed the one before, for each of them in
one go on the page's thread: 1.4 s in Chrome (software decoding, 44
microseconds a decoder) for a message of 90 KB on the wire, and as long
again for the next one. The page froze, and every session in it.
Opening decoders only at key frames would not help: a server can make
every frame one.

How fast decoders open is now bounded, for each surface and for the
session. A surface's decoders may open 16 times in a row, then 4 times a
second; the session's 128 times in a row, then 32 times a second.
Servers start a surface's stream again when they resize its monitor, a
few times in a row at most and not several times a second for long, and
a session has 16 monitors at most (MS-RDPBCGR 2.2.1.3.6): 128 in a row
is each of them resized eight times in a row. A decoder opening faster
than that fails the decoder, which ends the session as a decoder error
does. At 44 microseconds a decoder, the limits hold the page's thread
for under 6 ms in a row and under 1.5 ms a second after that.

A surface's count is kept while it matters and let go once its
allowance is all back, so the counts stay few however many surface ids
a server goes through.
